/home/yixunhu/.codex/sessions/2026/09/26/rollout-2026-09-26T17-38-09-01a0dfa7-77ae-7b52-839b-bcc58738f2e6.jsonl:58:{"timestamp":"2026-09-26T21:38:34.144Z","ordinal":57,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0dfa7-77ae-7b52-839b-bcc58738f2e6","turn_id":"01a0dfa7-77ce-7782-ac15-668bcbd12958","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-412ed7c9-b8ef-442a-8fa7-ba116a5684cd","process_id":"75002","command":["/bin/bash","-lc","rg -n \"acoustic_metrics_batch|logspec_mad|consistency|apply_delay|aligned|out0|pad_batch|per_sample_losses|log_mse|want_t60\" eval_yaw_rotation.py && nl -ba tools/yaw_rotation.py"],"cwd":"file:///home/yixunhu/codespace/xRIR_code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n 'acoustic_metrics_batch|logspec_mad|consistency|apply_delay|aligned|out0|pad_batch|per_sample_losses|log_mse|want_t60' eval_yaw_rotation.py","query":"acoustic_metrics_batch|logspec_mad|consistency|apply_delay|aligned|out0|pad_batch|per_sample_losses|log_mse|want_t60","path":"eval_yaw_rotation.py"},{"type":"read","cmd":"nl -ba tools/yaw_rotation.py","name":"yaw_rotation.py","path":"tools/yaw_rotation.py"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"24:``model.xRIR.apply_delay`` hard-codes ``.cuda()``, so the whole evaluation runs on GPU.\n47:    per_sample_losses,\n79:def forward_conditions(model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, k, aligned0):\n91:        aligned0: the ``k = 0`` aligned reference audio, i.e.\n102:        with fixed_alignment(model, aligned0):\n114:            reference for ``consistency``.\n118:        ``{\"loss\", \"stft\", \"decay\", \"log_mse\", \"consistency\"}``, each a detached CPU\n121:        * ``loss``/``stft``/``decay`` -- ``tools.per_sample_metrics.per_sample_losses``,\n123:        * ``log_mse`` -- the per-sample form of exp_01's\n125:        * ``consistency`` -- ``mean |out_k - out_0|`` over the log-spectrogram, exactly\n128:    loss, stft, decay = per_sample_losses(out_k, tgt_spec)\n131:        log_mse = ((out_k[..., 0] - log_tgt) ** 2).flatten(1).mean(dim=1)\n132:        consistency = (out_k - out_0).abs().flatten(1).mean(dim=1)\n134:            \"log_mse\": log_mse.detach().cpu().float(),\n135:            \"consistency\": consistency.detach().cpu().float()}\n138:def acoustic_metrics_batch(out_k, tgt_wav, query_keys, evaluator, gl_seed):\n209:def decomposition_at(model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, aligned0, k=32):\n210:    \"\"\"Where a patch-aligned yaw enters an ``xRIR_Cyl`` forward pass (diagnostic).\n212:    CylindricalViT's tokens are equivariant to patch-aligned azimuth rolls, but ``xRIR``\n226:        k: the patch-aligned angle to decompose (the plan's diagnostic angle is 32).\n263:        model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, 0, aligned0)[0]\n265:        model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, k, aligned0)[0]\n329:def pad_batch(batch, batch_size):\n384:        batch_size: the canonical compute shape (see :func:`pad_batch`).  A short batch\n394:    batch, n_real = pad_batch(batch, batch_size)\n405:        aligned0 = model.shift_and_align(ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs)\n415:            model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, k, aligned0)\n422:                    acoustic_0 = acoustic_metrics_batch(pred, tgt_real, keys, evaluator,\n426:                cell.update(acoustic_metrics_batch(pred, tgt_real, keys, evaluator, gl_seed))\n470:        aligned0 = model.shift_and_align(ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs)\n472:                            aligned0, k=k)\n535:    ``--batch-size`` rows (see :func:`pad_batch`), so a query's recorded values do not\n551:        RuntimeError: if no GPU is visible (``model.xRIR.apply_delay`` hard-codes ``.cuda()``).\n556:        raise RuntimeError(\"eval_yaw_rotation needs a GPU: xRIR.apply_delay is .cuda()-only\")\n     1\t\"\"\"Active yaw rotation of an xRIR scene about the receiver's vertical axis.\n     2\t\n     3\tExperiment exp_03 (``yaw_rotation_degradation``) asks whether jointly rotating the\n     4\treceiver-centred depth panorama and the source / reference-source coordinates in yaw\n     5\tdegrades RIR synthesis.  For an omnidirectional source and a monaural receiver this\n     6\trotation is a symmetry of the acoustics, so the ground-truth RIR is unchanged and any\n     7\tchange in the prediction is model sensitivity.\n     8\t\n     9\tConvention\n    10\t----------\n    11\tAn **active** yaw of the whole scene by ``+Delta`` about the receiver's ``z`` axis,\n    12\tright-handed (counter-clockwise seen from ``+z``: ``+x -> +y``), with\n    13\t\n    14\t    Delta = 2*pi*k / W\n    15\t\n    16\tfor an integer roll of ``k`` panorama columns (``W`` columns in total).  The panorama\n    17\tconvention is the dataset's ``convert_equirect_to_camera_coord``: column ``c`` looks\n    18\talong ``theta_c = (c + 0.5) * 2*pi/W - pi`` with ``x = d cos(phi) cos(theta)``,\n    19\t``y = d cos(phi) sin(theta)``, ``z = -d sin(phi)``.  Because ``theta_c - theta_{c-k} =\n    20\t2*pi*k/W = Delta``, rolling the panorama by ``+k`` columns and then rotating the xyz\n    21\tchannel vectors by ``Rz(Delta)`` is exactly the same scene as re-projecting the rolled\n    22\tdepth map, i.e. ``Rz(Delta) . roll(convert(D), k) == convert(roll(D, k))``.\n    23\t\n    24\tAll functions are pure (no in-place modification of their arguments) and work on CPU\n    25\tand CUDA tensors.\n    26\t\"\"\"\n    27\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n    28\t\n    29\timport contextlib\n    30\timport math\n    31\timport numbers\n    32\timport types\n    33\tfrom typing import Tuple\n    34\t\n    35\timport torch\n    36\t\n    37\t\n    38\tdef _check_k_and_width(k, W) -> None:\n    39\t    \"\"\"Validate a column roll ``k`` and a panorama width ``W``.\n    40\t\n    41\t    Raises:\n    42\t        TypeError: if ``k`` or ``W`` is not an integer (floats are rejected rather than\n    43\t            silently truncated, since a fractional roll has no meaning here).\n    44\t        ValueError: if ``W <= 0``.\n    45\t    \"\"\"\n    46\t    if isinstance(k, bool) or not isinstance(k, numbers.Integral):\n    47\t        raise TypeError(\"k must be an integer number of panorama columns, got {!r}\".format(k))\n    48\t    if isinstance(W, bool) or not isinstance(W, numbers.Integral):\n    49\t        raise TypeError(\"W must be an integer panorama width, got {!r}\".format(W))\n    50\t    if int(W) <= 0:\n    51\t        raise ValueError(\"W must be positive, got {}\".format(W))\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\tdef _require_float(t: torch.Tensor, name: str) -> None:\n    55\t    \"\"\"Raise ``TypeError`` unless ``t`` is a floating-point tensor.\"\"\"\n    56\t    if not torch.is_floating_point(t):\n    57\t        raise TypeError(\"{} must be a floating-point tensor, got dtype {}\".format(name, t.dtype))\n    58\t\n    59\t\n    60\tdef yaw_angle_rad(k: int, W: int = 512) -> float:\n    61\t    \"\"\"Yaw angle in radians corresponding to a roll of ``k`` panorama columns.\n    62\t\n    63\t    Args:\n    64\t        k: integer number of panorama columns; may be negative or ``>= W`` (not reduced\n    65\t            here).  Non-integer values raise ``TypeError``.\n    66\t        W: integer panorama width in columns; must be positive.\n    67\t\n    68\t    Returns:\n    69\t        ``2 * pi * k / W`` as a Python float.\n    70\t    \"\"\"\n    71\t    _check_k_and_width(k, W)\n    72\t    return 2.0 * math.pi * float(k) / float(W)\n    73\t\n    74\t\n    75\tdef rotate_vectors_z(v: torch.Tensor, angle: float) -> torch.Tensor:\n    76\t    \"\"\"Actively rotate 3-D vectors about the ``z`` axis by ``angle`` radians.\n    77\t\n    78\t    ``x' = x cos(a) - y sin(a)``, ``y' = x sin(a) + y cos(a)``, ``z' = z``.\n    79\t\n    80\t    Args:\n    81\t        v: floating-point tensor of shape ``[..., 3]``; the last axis holds ``(x, y, z)``.\n    82\t            Integer tensors are rejected (the rotation is not closed over the integers).\n    83\t        angle: rotation angle in radians (right-handed about ``+z``).\n    84\t\n    85\t    Returns:\n    86\t        A new tensor of the same shape, dtype and device as ``v``.\n    87\t\n    88\t    Raises:\n    89\t        TypeError: if ``v`` is not floating point.\n    90\t        ValueError: if the last axis of ``v`` is not of size 3.\n    91\t    \"\"\"\n    92\t    if v.shape[-1] != 3:\n    93\t        raise ValueError(\"rotate_vectors_z expects the last axis to be xyz (size 3), \"\n    94\t                         \"got shape {}\".format(tuple(v.shape)))\n    95\t    _require_float(v, \"v\")\n    96\t    cos_a = math.cos(angle)\n    97\t    sin_a = math.sin(angle)\n    98\t    x, y, z = v[..., 0], v[..., 1], v[..., 2]\n    99\t    return torch.stack([x * cos_a - y * sin_a, x * sin_a + y * cos_a, z], dim=-1)\n   100\t\n   101\t\n   102\tdef rotate_scene_yaw(\n   103\t    depth_coord: torch.Tensor,\n   104\t    src_loc: torch.Tensor,\n   105\t    ref_locs: torch.Tensor,\n   106\t    k: int,\n   107\t    W: int = 512,\n   108\t) -> Tuple[torch.Tensor, torch.Tensor, torch.Tensor]:\n   109\t    \"\"\"Apply the active ``+2*pi*k/W`` yaw to a whole xRIR scene.\n   110\t\n   111\t    ``depth_coord' = Rz(Delta) . roll(depth_coord, k, dim=-1)``,\n   112\t    ``src_loc' = Rz(Delta) src_loc``, ``ref_locs' = Rz(Delta) ref_locs``.\n   113\t\n   114\t    Args:\n   115\t        depth_coord: receiver-frame panorama coordinates ``[B, 3, H, W]``, floating point.\n   116\t        src_loc: receiver-frame query-source position ``[B, 3]``, floating point.\n   117\t        ref_locs: receiver-frame reference-source positions ``[B, K, 3]``, floating point.\n   118\t        k: integer column roll; negative values and values ``>= W`` are reduced modulo\n   119\t            ``W``.  Non-integer values raise ``TypeError``.\n   120\t        W: integer panorama width in columns (must match ``depth_coord.shape[-1]``).\n   121\t\n   122\t    Returns:\n   123\t        ``(depth_coord', src_loc', ref_locs')`` -- new tensors; the inputs are untouched.\n   124\t\n   125\t    Raises:\n   126\t        TypeError: if ``k``/``W`` are not integers or any tensor is not floating point.\n   127\t        ValueError: if ``W <= 0`` or the shapes do not match the contract.\n   128\t    \"\"\"\n   129\t    _check_k_and_width(k, W)\n   130\t    _require_float(depth_coord, \"depth_coord\")\n   131\t    _require_float(src_loc, \"src_loc\")\n   132\t    _require_float(ref_locs, \"ref_locs\")\n   133\t    if depth_coord.dim() != 4 or depth_coord.shape[1] != 3:\n   134\t        raise ValueError(\"depth_coord must have shape [B, 3, H, W], got {}\".format(\n   135\t            tuple(depth_coord.shape)))\n   136\t    if depth_coord.shape[-1] != W:\n   137\t        raise ValueError(\"depth_coord has {} columns but W={}\".format(\n   138\t            depth_coord.shape[-1], W))\n   139\t\n   140\t    k = int(k) % int(W)\n   141\t    angle = yaw_angle_rad(k, W)\n   142\t\n   143\t    rolled = torch.roll(depth_coord, shifts=k, dims=-1)          # [B, 3, H, W]\n   144\t    depth_rot = rotate_vectors_z(rolled.permute(0, 2, 3, 1), angle).permute(0, 3, 1, 2)\n   145\t    return depth_rot.contiguous(), rotate_vectors_z(src_loc, angle), rotate_vectors_z(ref_locs, angle)\n   146\t\n   147\t\n   148\tdef integer_delays(\n   149\t    src_loc: torch.Tensor,\n   150\t    ref_locs: torch.Tensor,\n   151\t    sr: int = 22050,\n   152\t    c: float = 343.0,\n   153\t) -> torch.Tensor:\n   154\t    \"\"\"Integer direct-path delays exactly as ``model.xRIR.xRIR.shift_and_align`` computes them.\n   155\t\n   156\t    The op sequence and dtypes mirror the model line for line\n   157\t    (``torch.linalg.norm`` -> ``torch.round(...).int()``), so both the values and the\n   158\t    dtype agree bit for bit with the model's ``delay_unit`` on the same inputs.\n   159\t\n   160\t    Args:\n   161\t        src_loc: receiver-frame query-source position ``[B, 3]``.\n   162\t        ref_locs: receiver-frame reference-source positions ``[B, K, 3]``.\n   163\t        sr: sample rate in Hz (the model hard-codes 22050).\n   164\t        c: speed of sound in m/s (the model hard-codes 343).\n   165\t\n   166\t    Returns:\n   167\t        ``[B, K]`` **int32** tensor of sample delays -- the dtype the model itself produces\n   168\t        (positive = reference arrives earlier than the query source and is therefore\n   169\t        shifted right).\n   170\t    \"\"\"\n   171\t    dist_src = torch.linalg.norm(src_loc, dim=1).unsqueeze(1)          # [B, 1]\n   172\t    dist_ref = torch.linalg.norm(ref_locs, dim=-1)                     # [B, K]\n   173\t    return torch.round((dist_src - dist_ref) / c * sr).int()           # as in shift_and_align\n   174\t\n   175\t\n   176\t@contextlib.contextmanager\n   177\tdef fixed_alignment(model, aligned_refs: torch.Tensor):\n   178\t    \"\"\"Temporarily pin ``model.shift_and_align`` to a pre-computed alignment.\n   179\t\n   180\t    ``xRIR.shift_and_align`` is algebraically yaw-invariant but not numerically: float32\n   181\t    norms plus ``round()`` can flip a delay by one sample under rotation.  The primary\n   182\t    experimental condition therefore holds the direct-path alignment at the ``k = 0``\n   183\t    coordinates so that only the geometry branches see the rotation::\n   184\t\n   185\t        aligned = model.shift_and_align(refs, src0, ref_locs0)\n   186\t        with fixed_alignment(model, aligned):\n   187\t            out, tgt = model(depth_k, refs, src_k, ref_locs_k, tgt)\n   188\t\n   189\t    The replacement is installed as a *bound method* of ``model`` (so ``model``'s own\n   190\t    ``forward`` sees the usual ``self.shift_and_align(x, src, ref)`` call signature), and\n   191\t    the original bound method is restored on exit, including when the block raises.\n   192\t\n   193\t    This mutates ``model`` for the duration of the block: it is for **serial, eager**\n   194\t    evaluation only and is **not thread-safe** -- do not share ``model`` across threads,\n   195\t    and do not let a lazily evaluated forward escape the block.\n   196\t\n   197\t    Args:\n   198\t        model: an ``xRIR`` (or subclass) instance.\n   199\t        aligned_refs: the cached ``[B, K, T]`` aligned reference audio to return instead.\n   200\t\n   201\t    Yields:\n   202\t        ``model``, with its ``shift_and_align`` replaced.\n   203\t    \"\"\"\n   204\t    had_own = \"shift_and_align\" in vars(model)\n   205\t    original = vars(model).get(\"shift_and_align\", None)\n   206\t\n   207\t    model.shift_and_align = types.MethodType(\n   208\t        lambda self, x, src_loc, ref_ir_locs: aligned_refs, model)\n   209\t    try:\n   210\t        yield model\n   211\t    finally:\n   212\t        if had_own:\n   213\t            model.shift_and_align = original\n   214\t        elif \"shift_and_align\" in vars(model):\n   215\t            # Guarded so a raise inside the block is never masked by an AttributeError here.\n   216\t            del model.shift_and_align\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"24:``model.xRIR.apply_delay`` hard-codes ``.cuda()``, so the whole evaluation runs on GPU.\n47:    per_sample_losses,\n79:def forward_conditions(model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, k, aligned0):\n91:        aligned0: the ``k = 0`` aligned reference audio, i.e.\n102:        with fixed_alignment(model, aligned0):\n114:            reference for ``consistency``.\n118:        ``{\"loss\", \"stft\", \"decay\", \"log_mse\", \"consistency\"}``, each a detached CPU\n121:        * ``loss``/``stft``/``decay`` -- ``tools.per_sample_metrics.per_sample_losses``,\n123:        * ``log_mse`` -- the per-sample form of exp_01's\n125:        * ``consistency`` -- ``mean |out_k - out_0|`` over the log-spectrogram, exactly\n128:    loss, stft, decay = per_sample_losses(out_k, tgt_spec)\n131:        log_mse = ((out_k[..., 0] - log_tgt) ** 2).flatten(1).mean(dim=1)\n132:        consistency = (out_k - out_0).abs().flatten(1).mean(dim=1)\n134:            \"log_mse\": log_mse.detach().cpu().float(),\n135:            \"consistency\": consistency.detach().cpu().float()}\n138:def acoustic_metrics_batch(out_k, tgt_wav, query_keys, evaluator, gl_seed):\n209:def decomposition_at(model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, aligned0, k=32):\n210:    \"\"\"Where a patch-aligned yaw enters an ``xRIR_Cyl`` forward pass (diagnostic).\n212:    CylindricalViT's tokens are equivariant to patch-aligned azimuth rolls, but ``xRIR``\n226:        k: the patch-aligned angle to decompose (the plan's diagnostic angle is 32).\n263:        model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, 0, aligned0)[0]\n265:        model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, k, aligned0)[0]\n329:def pad_batch(batch, batch_size):\n384:        batch_size: the canonical compute shape (see :func:`pad_batch`).  A short batch\n394:    batch, n_real = pad_batch(batch, batch_size)\n405:        aligned0 = model.shift_and_align(ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs)\n415:            model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, k, aligned0)\n422:                    acoustic_0 = acoustic_metrics_batch(pred, tgt_real, keys, evaluator,\n426:                cell.update(acoustic_metrics_batch(pred, tgt_real, keys, evaluator, gl_seed))\n470:        aligned0 = model.shift_and_align(ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs)\n472:                            aligned0, k=k)\n535:    ``--batch-size`` rows (see :func:`pad_batch`), so a query's recorded values do not\n551:        RuntimeError: if no GPU is visible (``model.xRIR.apply_delay`` hard-codes ``.cuda()``).\n556:        raise RuntimeError(\"eval_yaw_rotation needs a GPU: xRIR.apply_delay is .cuda()-only\")\n     1\t\"\"\"Active yaw rotation of an xRIR scene about the receiver's vertical axis.\n     2\t\n     3\tExperiment exp_03 (``yaw_rotation_degradation``) asks whether jointly rotating the\n     4\treceiver-centred depth panorama and the source / reference-source coordinates in yaw\n     5\tdegrades RIR synthesis.  For an omnidirectional source and a monaural receiver this\n     6\trotation is a symmetry of the acoustics, so the ground-truth RIR is unchanged and any\n     7\tchange in the prediction is model sensitivity.\n     8\t\n     9\tConvention\n    10\t----------\n    11\tAn **active** yaw of the whole scene by ``+Delta`` about the receiver's ``z`` axis,\n    12\tright-handed (counter-clockwise seen from ``+z``: ``+x -> +y``), with\n    13\t\n    14\t    Delta = 2*pi*k / W\n    15\t\n    16\tfor an integer roll of ``k`` panorama columns (``W`` columns in total).  The panorama\n    17\tconvention is the dataset's ``convert_equirect_to_camera_coord``: column ``c`` looks\n    18\talong ``theta_c = (c + 0.5) * 2*pi/W - pi`` with ``x = d cos(phi) cos(theta)``,\n    19\t``y = d cos(phi) sin(theta)``, ``z = -d sin(phi)``.  Because ``theta_c - theta_{c-k} =\n    20\t2*pi*k/W = Delta``, rolling the panorama by ``+k`` columns and then rotating the xyz\n    21\tchannel vectors by ``Rz(Delta)`` is exactly the same scene as re-projecting the rolled\n    22\tdepth map, i.e. ``Rz(Delta) . roll(convert(D), k) == convert(roll(D, k))``.\n    23\t\n    24\tAll functions are pure (no in-place modification of their arguments) and work on CPU\n    25\tand CUDA tensors.\n    26\t\"\"\"\n    27\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n    28\t\n    29\timport contextlib\n    30\timport math\n    31\timport numbers\n    32\timport types\n    33\tfrom typing import Tuple\n    34\t\n    35\timport torch\n    36\t\n    37\t\n    38\tdef _check_k_and_width(k, W) -> None:\n    39\t    \"\"\"Validate a column roll ``k`` and a panorama width ``W``.\n    40\t\n    41\t    Raises:\n    42\t        TypeError: if ``k`` or ``W`` is not an integer (floats are rejected rather than\n    43\t            silently truncated, since a fractional roll has no meaning here).\n    44\t        ValueError: if ``W <= 0``.\n    45\t    \"\"\"\n    46\t    if isinstance(k, bool) or not isinstance(k, numbers.Integral):\n    47\t        raise TypeError(\"k must be an integer number of panorama columns, got {!r}\".format(k))\n    48\t    if isinstance(W, bool) or not isinstance(W, numbers.Integral):\n    49\t        raise TypeError(\"W must be an integer panorama width, got {!r}\".format(W))\n    50\t    if int(W) <= 0:\n    51\t        raise ValueError(\"W must be positive, got {}\".format(W))\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\tdef _require_float(t: torch.Tensor, name: str) -> None:\n    55\t    \"\"\"Raise ``TypeError`` unless ``t`` is a floating-point tensor.\"\"\"\n    56\t    if not torch.is_floating_point(t):\n    57\t        raise TypeError(\"{} must be a floating-point tensor, got dtype {}\".format(name, t.dtype))\n    58\t\n    59\t\n    60\tdef yaw_angle_rad(k: int, W: int = 512) -> float:\n    61\t    \"\"\"Yaw angle in radians corresponding to a roll of ``k`` panorama columns.\n    62\t\n    63\t    Args:\n    64\t        k: integer number of panorama columns; may be negative or ``>= W`` (not reduced\n    65\t            here).  Non-integer values raise ``TypeError``.\n    66\t        W: integer panorama width in columns; must be positive.\n    67\t\n    68\t    Returns:\n    69\t        ``2 * pi * k / W`` as a Python float.\n    70\t    \"\"\"\n    71\t    _check_k_and_width(k, W)\n    72\t    return 2.0 * math.pi * float(k) / float(W)\n    73\t\n    74\t\n    75\tdef rotate_vectors_z(v: torch.Tensor, angle: float) -> torch.Tensor:\n    76\t    \"\"\"Actively rotate 3-D vectors about the ``z`` axis by ``angle`` radians.\n    77\t\n    78\t    ``x' = x cos(a) - y sin(a)``, ``y' = x sin(a) + y cos(a)``, ``z' = z``.\n    79\t\n    80\t    Args:\n    81\t        v: floating-point tensor of shape ``[..., 3]``; the last axis holds ``(x, y, z)``.\n    82\t            Integer tensors are rejected (the rotation is not closed over the integers).\n    83\t        angle: rotation angle in radians (right-handed about ``+z``).\n    84\t\n    85\t    Returns:\n    86\t        A new tensor of the same shape, dtype and device as ``v``.\n    87\t\n    88\t    Raises:\n    89\t        TypeError: if ``v`` is not floating point.\n    90\t        ValueError: if the last axis of ``v`` is not of size 3.\n    91\t    \"\"\"\n    92\t    if v.shape[-1] != 3:\n    93\t        raise ValueError(\"rotate_vectors_z expects the last axis to be xyz (size 3), \"\n    94\t                         \"got shape {}\".format(tuple(v.shape)))\n    95\t    _require_float(v, \"v\")\n    96\t    cos_a = math.cos(angle)\n    97\t    sin_a = math.sin(angle)\n    98\t    x, y, z = v[..., 0], v[..., 1], v[..., 2]\n    99\t    return torch.stack([x * cos_a - y * sin_a, x * sin_a + y * cos_a, z], dim=-1)\n   100\t\n   101\t\n   102\tdef rotate_scene_yaw(\n   103\t    depth_coord: torch.Tensor,\n   104\t    src_loc: torch.Tensor,\n   105\t    ref_locs: torch.Tensor,\n   106\t    k: int,\n   107\t    W: int = 512,\n   108\t) -> Tuple[torch.Tensor, torch.Tensor, torch.Tensor]:\n   109\t    \"\"\"Apply the active ``+2*pi*k/W`` yaw to a whole xRIR scene.\n   110\t\n   111\t    ``depth_coord' = Rz(Delta) . roll(depth_coord, k, dim=-1)``,\n   112\t    ``src_loc' = Rz(Delta) src_loc``, ``ref_locs' = Rz(Delta) ref_locs``.\n   113\t\n   114\t    Args:\n   115\t        depth_coord: receiver-frame panorama coordinates ``[B, 3, H, W]``, floating point.\n   116\t        src_loc: receiver-frame query-source position ``[B, 3]``, floating point.\n   117\t        ref_locs: receiver-frame reference-source positions ``[B, K, 3]``, floating point.\n   118\t        k: integer column roll; negative values and values ``>= W`` are reduced modulo\n   119\t            ``W``.  Non-integer values raise ``TypeError``.\n   120\t        W: integer panorama width in columns (must match ``depth_coord.shape[-1]``).\n   121\t\n   122\t    Returns:\n   123\t        ``(depth_coord', src_loc', ref_locs')`` -- new tensors; the inputs are untouched.\n   124\t\n   125\t    Raises:\n   126\t        TypeError: if ``k``/``W`` are not integers or any tensor is not floating point.\n   127\t        ValueError: if ``W <= 0`` or the shapes do not match the contract.\n   128\t    \"\"\"\n   129\t    _check_k_and_width(k, W)\n   130\t    _require_float(depth_coord, \"depth_coord\")\n   131\t    _require_float(src_loc, \"src_loc\")\n   132\t    _require_float(ref_locs, \"ref_locs\")\n   133\t    if depth_coord.dim() != 4 or depth_coord.shape[1] != 3:\n   134\t        raise ValueError(\"depth_coord must have shape [B, 3, H, W], got {}\".format(\n   135\t            tuple(depth_coord.shape)))\n   136\t    if depth_coord.shape[-1] != W:\n   137\t        raise ValueError(\"depth_coord has {} columns but W={}\".format(\n   138\t            depth_coord.shape[-1], W))\n   139\t\n   140\t    k = int(k) % int(W)\n   141\t    angle = yaw_angle_rad(k, W)\n   142\t\n   143\t    rolled = torch.roll(depth_coord, shifts=k, dims=-1)          # [B, 3, H, W]\n   144\t    depth_rot = rotate_vectors_z(rolled.permute(0, 2, 3, 1), angle).permute(0, 3, 1, 2)\n   145\t    return depth_rot.contiguous(), rotate_vectors_z(src_loc, angle), rotate_vectors_z(ref_locs, angle)\n   146\t\n   147\t\n   148\tdef integer_delays(\n   149\t    src_loc: torch.Tensor,\n   150\t    ref_locs: torch.Tensor,\n   151\t    sr: int = 22050,\n   152\t    c: float = 343.0,\n   153\t) -> torch.Tensor:\n   154\t    \"\"\"Integer direct-path delays exactly as ``model.xRIR.xRIR.shift_and_align`` computes them.\n   155\t\n   156\t    The op sequence and dtypes mirror the model line for line\n   157\t    (``torch.linalg.norm`` -> ``torch.round(...).int()``), so both the values and the\n   158\t    dtype agree bit for bit with the model's ``delay_unit`` on the same inputs.\n   159\t\n   160\t    Args:\n   161\t        src_loc: receiver-frame query-source position ``[B, 3]``.\n   162\t        ref_locs: receiver-frame reference-source positions ``[B, K, 3]``.\n   163\t        sr: sample rate in Hz (the model hard-codes 22050).\n   164\t        c: speed of sound in m/s (the model hard-codes 343).\n   165\t\n   166\t    Returns:\n   167\t        ``[B, K]`` **int32** tensor of sample delays -- the dtype the model itself produces\n   168\t        (positive = reference arrives earlier than the query source and is therefore\n   169\t        shifted right).\n   170\t    \"\"\"\n   171\t    dist_src = torch.linalg.norm(src_loc, dim=1).unsqueeze(1)          # [B, 1]\n   172\t    dist_ref = torch.linalg.norm(ref_locs, dim=-1)                     # [B, K]\n   173\t    return torch.round((dist_src - dist_ref) / c * sr).int()           # as in shift_and_align\n   174\t\n   175\t\n   176\t@contextlib.contextmanager\n   177\tdef fixed_alignment(model, aligned_refs: torch.Tensor):\n   178\t    \"\"\"Temporarily pin ``model.shift_and_align`` to a pre-computed alignment.\n   179\t\n   180\t    ``xRIR.shift_and_align`` is algebraically yaw-invariant but not numerically: float32\n   181\t    norms plus ``round()`` can flip a delay by one sample under rotation.  The primary\n   182\t    experimental condition therefore holds the direct-path alignment at the ``k = 0``\n   183\t    coordinates so that only the geometry branches see the rotation::\n   184\t\n   185\t        aligned = model.shift_and_align(refs, src0, ref_locs0)\n   186\t        with fixed_alignment(model, aligned):\n   187\t            out, tgt = model(depth_k, refs, src_k, ref_locs_k, tgt)\n   188\t\n   189\t    The replacement is installed as a *bound method* of ``model`` (so ``model``'s own\n   190\t    ``forward`` sees the usual ``self.shift_and_align(x, src, ref)`` call signature), and\n   191\t    the original bound method is restored on exit, including when the block raises.\n   192\t\n   193\t    This mutates ``model`` for the duration of the block: it is for **serial, eager**\n   194\t    evaluation only and is **not thread-safe** -- do not share ``model`` across threads,\n   195\t    and do not let a lazily evaluated forward escape the block.\n   196\t\n   197\t    Args:\n   198\t        model: an ``xRIR`` (or subclass) instance.\n   199\t        aligned_refs: the cached ``[B, K, T]`` aligned reference audio to return instead.\n   200\t\n   201\t    Yields:\n   202\t        ``model``, with its ``shift_and_align`` replaced.\n   203\t    \"\"\"\n   204\t    had_own = \"shift_and_align\" in vars(model)\n   205\t    original = vars(model).get(\"shift_and_align\", None)\n   206\t\n   207\t    model.shift_and_align = types.MethodType(\n   208\t        lambda self, x, src_loc, ref_ir_locs: aligned_refs, model)\n   209\t    try:\n   210\t        yield model\n   211\t    finally:\n   212\t        if had_own:\n   213\t            model.shift_and_align = original\n   214\t        elif \"shift_and_align\" in vars(model):\n   215\t            # Guarded so a raise inside the block is never masked by an AttributeError here.\n   216\t            del model.shift_and_align\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2791},"formatted_output":"24:``model.xRIR.apply_delay`` hard-codes ``.cuda()``, so the whole evaluation runs on GPU.\n47:    per_sample_losses,\n79:def forward_conditions(model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, k, aligned0):\n91:        aligned0: the ``k = 0`` aligned reference audio, i.e.\n102:        with fixed_alignment(model, aligned0):\n114:            reference for ``consistency``.\n118:        ``{\"loss\", \"stft\", \"decay\", \"log_mse\", \"consistency\"}``, each a detached CPU\n121:        * ``loss``/``stft``/``decay`` -- ``tools.per_sample_metrics.per_sample_losses``,\n123:        * ``log_mse`` -- the per-sample form of exp_01's\n125:        * ``consistency`` -- ``mean |out_k - out_0|`` over the log-spectrogram, exactly\n128:    loss, stft, decay = per_sample_losses(out_k, tgt_spec)\n131:        log_mse = ((out_k[..., 0] - log_tgt) ** 2).flatten(1).mean(dim=1)\n132:        consistency = (out_k - out_0).abs().flatten(1).mean(dim=1)\n134:            \"log_mse\": log_mse.detach().cpu().float(),\n135:            \"consistency\": consistency.detach().cpu().float()}\n138:def acoustic_metrics_batch(out_k, tgt_wav, query_keys, evaluator, gl_seed):\n209:def decomposition_at(model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, aligned0, k=32):\n210:    \"\"\"Where a patch-aligned yaw enters an ``xRIR_Cyl`` forward pass (diagnostic).\n212:    CylindricalViT's tokens are equivariant to patch-aligned azimuth rolls, but ``xRIR``\n226:        k: the patch-aligned angle to decompose (the plan's diagnostic angle is 32).\n263:        model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, 0, aligned0)[0]\n265:        model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, k, aligned0)[0]\n329:def pad_batch(batch, batch_size):\n384:        batch_size: the canonical compute shape (see :func:`pad_batch`).  A short batch\n394:    batch, n_real = pad_batch(batch, batch_size)\n405:        aligned0 = model.shift_and_align(ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs)\n415:            model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, k, aligned0)\n422:                    acoustic_0 = acoustic_metrics_batch(pred, tgt_real, keys, evaluator,\n426:                cell.update(acoustic_metrics_batch(pred, tgt_real, keys, evaluator, gl_seed))\n470:        aligned0 = model.shift_and_align(ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs)\n472:                            aligned0, k=k)\n535:    ``--batch-size`` rows (see :func:`pad_batch`), so a query's recorded values do not\n551:        RuntimeError: if no GPU is visible (``model.xRIR.apply_delay`` hard-codes ``.cuda()``).\n556:        raise RuntimeError(\"eval_yaw_rotation needs a GPU: xRIR.apply_delay is .cuda()-only\")\n     1\t\"\"\"Active yaw rotation of an xRIR scene about the receiver's vertical axis.\n     2\t\n     3\tExperiment exp_03 (``yaw_rotation_degradation``) asks whether jointly rotating the\n     4\treceiver-centred depth panorama and the source / reference-source coordinates in yaw\n     5\tdegrades RIR synthesis.  For an omnidirectional source and a monaural receiver this\n     6\trotation is a symmetry of the acoustics, so the ground-truth RIR is unchanged and any\n     7\tchange in the prediction is model sensitivity.\n     8\t\n     9\tConvention\n    10\t----------\n    11\tAn **active** yaw of the whole scene by ``+Delta`` about the receiver's ``z`` axis,\n    12\tright-handed (counter-clockwise seen from ``+z``: ``+x -> +y``), with\n    13\t\n    14\t    Delta = 2*pi*k / W\n    15\t\n    16\tfor an integer roll of ``k`` panorama columns (``W`` columns in total).  The panorama\n    17\tconvention is the dataset's ``convert_equirect_to_camera_coord``: column ``c`` looks\n    18\talong ``theta_c = (c + 0.5) * 2*pi/W - pi`` with ``x = d cos(phi) cos(theta)``,\n    19\t``y = d cos(phi) sin(theta)``, ``z = -d sin(phi)``.  Because ``theta_c - theta_{c-k} =\n    20\t2*pi*k/W = Delta``, rolling the panorama by ``+k`` columns and then rotating the xyz\n    21\tchannel vectors by ``Rz(Delta)`` is exactly the same scene as re-projecting the rolled\n    22\tdepth map, i.e. ``Rz(Delta) . roll(convert(D), k) == convert(roll(D, k))``.\n    23\t\n    24\tAll functions are pure (no in-place modification of their arguments) and work on CPU\n    25\tand CUDA tensors.\n    26\t\"\"\"\n    27\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n    28\t\n    29\timport contextlib\n    30\timport math\n    31\timport numbers\n    32\timport types\n    33\tfrom typing import Tuple\n    34\t\n    35\timport torch\n    36\t\n    37\t\n    38\tdef _check_k_and_width(k, W) -> None:\n    39\t    \"\"\"Validate a column roll ``k`` and a panorama width ``W``.\n    40\t\n    41\t    Raises:\n    42\t        TypeError: if ``k`` or ``W`` is not an integer (floats are rejected rather than\n    43\t            silently truncated, since a fractional roll has no meaning here).\n    44\t        ValueError: if ``W <= 0``.\n    45\t    \"\"\"\n    46\t    if isinstance(k, bool) or not isinstance(k, numbers.Integral):\n    47\t        raise TypeError(\"k must be an integer number of panorama columns, got {!r}\".format(k))\n    48\t    if isinstance(W, bool) or not isinstance(W, numbers.Integral):\n    49\t        raise TypeError(\"W must be an integer panorama width, got {!r}\".format(W))\n    50\t    if int(W) <= 0:\n    51\t        raise ValueError(\"W must be positive, got {}\".format(W))\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\tdef _require_float(t: torch.Tensor, name: str) -> None:\n    55\t    \"\"\"Raise ``TypeError`` unless ``t`` is a floating-point tensor.\"\"\"\n    56\t    if not torch.is_floating_point(t):\n    57\t        raise TypeError(\"{} must be a floating-point tensor, got dtype {}\".format(name, t.dtype))\n    58\t\n    59\t\n    60\tdef yaw_angle_rad(k: int, W: int = 512) -> float:\n    61\t    \"\"\"Yaw angle in radians corresponding to a roll of ``k`` panorama columns.\n    62\t\n    63\t    Args:\n    64\t        k: integer number of panorama columns; may be negative or ``>= W`` (not reduced\n    65\t            here).  Non-integer values raise ``TypeError``.\n    66\t        W: integer panorama width in columns; must be positive.\n    67\t\n    68\t    Returns:\n    69\t        ``2 * pi * k / W`` as a Python float.\n    70\t    \"\"\"\n    71\t    _check_k_and_width(k, W)\n    72\t    return 2.0 * math.pi * float(k) / float(W)\n    73\t\n    74\t\n    75\tdef rotate_vectors_z(v: torch.Tensor, angle: float) -> torch.Tensor:\n    76\t    \"\"\"Actively rotate 3-D vectors about the ``z`` axis by ``angle`` radians.\n    77\t\n    78\t    ``x' = x cos(a) - y sin(a)``, ``y' = x sin(a) + y cos(a)``, ``z' = z``.\n    79\t\n    80\t    Args:\n    81\t        v: floating-point tensor of shape ``[..., 3]``; the last axis holds ``(x, y, z)``.\n    82\t            Integer tensors are rejected (the rotation is not closed over the integers).\n    83\t        angle: rotation angle in radians (right-handed about ``+z``).\n    84\t\n    85\t    Returns:\n    86\t        A new tensor of the same shape, dtype and device as ``v``.\n    87\t\n    88\t    Raises:\n    89\t        TypeError: if ``v`` is not floating point.\n    90\t        ValueError: if the last axis of ``v`` is not of size 3.\n    91\t    \"\"\"\n    92\t    if v.shape[-1] != 3:\n    93\t        raise ValueError(\"rotate_vectors_z expects the last axis to be xyz (size 3), \"\n    94\t                         \"got shape {}\".format(tuple(v.shape)))\n    95\t    _require_float(v, \"v\")\n    96\t    cos_a = math.cos(angle)\n    97\t    sin_a = math.sin(angle)\n    98\t    x, y, z = v[..., 0], v[..., 1], v[..., 2]\n    99\t    return torch.stack([x * cos_a - y * sin_a, x * sin_a + y * cos_a, z], dim=-1)\n   100\t\n   101\t\n   102\tdef rotate_scene_yaw(\n   103\t    depth_coord: torch.Tensor,\n   104\t    src_loc: torch.Tensor,\n   105\t    ref_locs: torch.Tensor,\n   106\t    k: int,\n   107\t    W: int = 512,\n   108\t) -> Tuple[torch.Tensor, torch.Tensor, torch.Tensor]:\n   109\t    \"\"\"Apply the active ``+2*pi*k/W`` yaw to a whole xRIR scene.\n   110\t\n   111\t    ``depth_coord' = Rz(Delta) . roll(depth_coord, k, dim=-1)``,\n   112\t    ``src_loc' = Rz(Delta) src_loc``, ``ref_locs' = Rz(Delta) ref_locs``.\n   113\t\n   114\t    Args:\n   115\t        depth_coord: receiver-frame panorama coordinates ``[B, 3, H, W]``, floating point.\n   116\t        src_loc: receiver-frame query-source position ``[B, 3]``, floating point.\n   117\t        ref_locs: receiver-frame reference-source positions ``[B, K, 3]``, floating point.\n   118\t        k: integer column roll; negative values and values ``>= W`` are reduced modulo\n   119\t            ``W``.  Non-integer values raise ``TypeError``.\n   120\t        W: integer panorama width in columns (must match ``depth_coord.shape[-1]``).\n   121\t\n   122\t    Returns:\n   123\t        ``(depth_coord', src_loc', ref_locs')`` -- new tensors; the inputs are untouched.\n   124\t\n   125\t    Raises:\n   126\t        TypeError: if ``k``/``W`` are not integers or any tensor is not floating point.\n   127\t        ValueError: if ``W <= 0`` or the shapes do not match the contract.\n   128\t    \"\"\"\n   129\t    _check_k_and_width(k, W)\n   130\t    _require_float(depth_coord, \"depth_coord\")\n   131\t    _require_float(src_loc, \"src_loc\")\n   132\t    _require_float(ref_locs, \"ref_locs\")\n   133\t    if depth_coord.dim() != 4 or depth_coord.shape[1] != 3:\n   134\t        raise ValueError(\"depth_coord must have shape [B, 3, H, W], got {}\".format(\n   135\t            tuple(depth_coord.shape)))\n   136\t    if depth_coord.shape[-1] != W:\n   137\t        raise ValueError(\"depth_coord has {} columns but W={}\".format(\n   138\t            depth_coord.shape[-1], W))\n   139\t\n   140\t    k = int(k) % int(W)\n   141\t    angle = yaw_angle_rad(k, W)\n   142\t\n   143\t    rolled = torch.roll(depth_coord, shifts=k, dims=-1)          # [B, 3, H, W]\n   144\t    depth_rot = rotate_vectors_z(rolled.permute(0, 2, 3, 1), angle).permute(0, 3, 1, 2)\n   145\t    return depth_rot.contiguous(), rotate_vectors_z(src_loc, angle), rotate_vectors_z(ref_locs, angle)\n   146\t\n   147\t\n   148\tdef integer_delays(\n   149\t    src_loc: torch.Tensor,\n   150\t    ref_locs: torch.Tensor,\n   151\t    sr: int = 22050,\n   152\t    c: float = 343.0,\n   153\t) -> torch.Tensor:\n   154\t    \"\"\"Integer direct-path delays exactly as ``model.xRIR.xRIR.shift_and_align`` computes them.\n   155\t\n   156\t    The op sequence and dtypes mirror the model line for line\n   157\t    (``torch.linalg.norm`` -> ``torch.round(...).int()``), so both the values and the\n   158\t    dtype agree bit for bit with the model's ``delay_unit`` on the same inputs.\n   159\t\n   160\t    Args:\n   161\t        src_loc: receiver-frame query-source position ``[B, 3]``.\n   162\t        ref_locs: receiver-frame reference-source positions ``[B, K, 3]``.\n   163\t        sr: sample rate in Hz (the model hard-codes 22050).\n   164\t        c: speed of sound in m/s (the model hard-codes 343).\n   165\t\n   166\t    Returns:\n   167\t        ``[B, K]`` **int32** tensor of sample delays -- the dtype the model itself produces\n   168\t        (positive = reference arrives earlier than the query source and is therefore\n   169\t        shifted right).\n   170\t    \"\"\"\n   171\t    dist_src = torch.linalg.norm(src_loc, dim=1).unsqueeze(1)          # [B, 1]\n   172\t    dist_ref = torch.linalg.norm(ref_locs, dim=-1)                     # [B, K]\n   173\t    return torch.round((dist_src - dist_ref) / c * sr).int()           # as in shift_and_align\n   174\t\n   175\t\n   176\t@contextlib.contextmanager\n   177\tdef fixed_alignment(model, aligned_refs: torch.Tensor):\n   178\t    \"\"\"Temporarily pin ``model.shift_and_align`` to a pre-computed alignment.\n   179\t\n   180\t    ``xRIR.shift_and_align`` is algebraically yaw-invariant but not numerically: float32\n   181\t    norms plus ``round()`` can flip a delay by one sample under rotation.  The primary\n   182\t    experimental condition therefore holds the direct-path alignment at the ``k = 0``\n   183\t    coordinates so that only the geometry branches see the rotation::\n   184\t\n   185\t        aligned = model.shift_and_align(refs, src0, ref_locs0)\n   186\t        with fixed_alignment(model, aligned):\n   187\t            out, tgt = model(depth_k, refs, src_k, ref_locs_k, tgt)\n   188\t\n   189\t    The replacement is installed as a *bound method* of ``model`` (so ``model``'s own\n   190\t    ``forward`` sees the usual ``self.shift_and_align(x, src, ref)`` call signature), and\n   191\t    the original bound method is restored on exit, including when the block raises.\n   192\t\n   193\t    This mutates ``model`` for the duration of the block: it is for **serial, eager**\n   194\t    evaluation only and is **not thread-safe** -- do not share ``model`` across threads,\n   195\t    and do not let a lazily evaluated forward escape the block.\n   196\t\n   197\t    Args:\n   198\t        model: an ``xRIR`` (or subclass) instance.\n   199\t        aligned_refs: the cached ``[B, K, T]`` aligned reference audio to return instead.\n   200\t\n   201\t    Yields:\n   202\t        ``model``, with its ``shift_and_align`` replaced.\n   203\t    \"\"\"\n   204\t    had_own = \"shift_and_align\" in vars(model)\n   205\t    original = vars(model).get(\"shift_and_align\", None)\n   206\t\n   207\t    model.shift_and_align = types.MethodType(\n   208\t        lambda self, x, src_loc, ref_ir_locs: aligned_refs, model)\n   209\t    try:\n   210\t        yield model\n   211\t    finally:\n   212\t        if had_own:\n   213\t            model.shift_and_align = original\n   214\t        elif \"shift_and_align\" in vars(model):\n   215\t            # Guarded so a raise inside the block is never masked by an AttributeError here.\n   216\t            del model.shift_and_align\n"},"started_at_ms":1790458714144,"completed_at_ms":1790458714144}}
/home/yixunhu/.codex/sessions/2026/09/26/rollout-2026-09-26T17-38-09-01a0dfa7-77ae-7b52-839b-bcc58738f2e6.jsonl:60:{"timestamp":"2026-09-26T21:38:34.152Z","ordinal":59,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0dfa7-d828-7bd0-8214-eae4062b0be2","call_id":"call_qv3BJ78G0dFSRqihXflV37bl","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"label\":\"flac_comp\",\"output\":\"     1\\t\\\"\\\"\\\"Offline Metric-1 (invariance gap) comparison between two stored prediction tensors.\\n     2\\t\\n     3\\tBoth ``.pt`` files are produced by ``eval_FLAC.py --store_predictions`` (the\\n     4\\t``decoded_samples_all`` tensor, saved at ``eval_FLAC.py:192-194``): a single\\n     5\\t``[N, 1, T]`` float32 CPU tensor of *decoded, un-clamped* RIR waveforms in\\n     6\\tdataloader order. A reference run (``P_ref``, unrotated baseline) and an\\n     7\\talternative run (``P_alt``, yaw-rotated conditioning) share dataset config,\\n     8\\tbatch size and seed, so sample ``i`` corresponds across the two files.\\n     9\\t\\n    10\\tReported quantities:\\n    11\\t\\n    12\\t* Waveform gap: per-sample mean-absolute-difference and relative L2\\n    13\\t  ``||alt - ref|| / (||ref|| + 1e-8)`` over the common minimum length, averaged\\n    14\\t  over the dataset.\\n    15\\t* Acoustic gap: T60 / C50 / EDT from :class:`AcousticMetricsCallback`, treating\\n    16\\t  ``P_ref`` as the ground truth and ``P_alt`` as the prediction (e.g. a relative\\n    17\\t  T60 error of ``alt`` w.r.t. ``ref``). FD / retrieval / env stay disabled so no\\n    18\\t  AGREE checkpoint is needed.\\n    19\\t* Exact-match: max absolute difference between the two tensors, the\\n    20\\t  rot0-vs-baseline determinism control (should be ~0 for identical runs).\\n    21\\t\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    22\\timport argparse\\n    23\\timport json\\n    24\\timport sys\\n    25\\tfrom pathlib import Path\\n    26\\tfrom typing import Any, Dict\\n    27\\t\\n    28\\t# The repo ships a `src` package that also has a stale copy installed in\\n    29\\t# site-packages; prepend the real repo root (three levels up:\\n    30\\t# worklog/exp_02_.../compare_predictions.py -> repo root) so `src.*` resolves\\n    31\\t# to the working tree, not the stale install.\\n    32\\tREPO_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]\\n    33\\tif str(REPO_ROOT) not in sys.path:\\n    34\\t    sys.path.insert(0, str(REPO_ROOT))\\n    35\\t\\n    36\\timport torch  # noqa: E402\\n    37\\t\\n    38\\tfrom src.metrics.metric_callback import AcousticMetricsCallback  # noqa: E402\\n    39\\t\\n    40\\t\\n    41\\tdef _to_float(value: Any) -> Any:\\n    42\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"Convert a scalar tensor to a Python float, passing other types through.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    43\\t    if isinstance(value, torch.Tensor):\\n    44\\t        return value.item() if value.numel() == 1 else value.tolist()\\n    45\\t    return value\\n    46\\t\\n    47\\t\\n    48\\tdef waveform_gap(ref: torch.Tensor, alt: torch.Tensor, batch_size: int) -> Dict[str, float]:\\n    49\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"Per-sample waveform mean-abs-diff and relative L2, averaged over the dataset.\\n    50\\t\\n    51\\t    Parameters\\n    52\\t    ----------\\n    53\\t    ref, alt : torch.Tensor\\n    54\\t        Prediction tensors ``[N, 1, T]`` (already trimmed to a common length).\\n    55\\t    batch_size : int\\n    56\\t        Mini-batch size used to bound memory.\\n    57\\t\\n    58\\t    Returns\\n    59\\t    -------\\n    60\\t    dict\\n    61\\t        ``mean_abs_diff``, ``mean_rel_l2`` and ``max_abs_diff`` over all samples.\\n    62\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    63\\t    n = ref.shape[0]\\n    64\\t    mad_sum, rel_l2_sum, max_abs = 0.0, 0.0, 0.0\\n    65\\t    for start in range(0, n, batch_size):\\n    66\\t        r = ref[start:start + batch_size].float().flatten(1)\\n    67\\t        a = alt[start:start + batch_size].float().flatten(1)\\n    68\\t        diff = a - r\\n    69\\t        mad_sum += diff.abs().mean(dim=1).sum().item()\\n    70\\t        rel_l2_sum += (diff.norm(dim=1) / (r.norm(dim=1) + 1e-8)).sum().item()\\n    71\\t        max_abs = max(max_abs, diff.abs().max().item())\\n    72\\t    return {\\n    73\\t        \\\"mean_abs_diff\\\": mad_sum / n,\\n    74\\t        \\\"mean_rel_l2\\\": rel_l2_sum / n,\\n    75\\t        \\\"max_abs_diff\\\": max_abs,\\n    76\\t    }\\n    77\\t\\n    78\\t\\n    79\\tdef acoustic_gap(\\n    80\\t    ref: torch.Tensor, alt: torch.Tensor, batch_size: int, sample_rate: int, device: str\\n    81\\t) -> Dict[str, Any]:\\n    82\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"T60 / C50 / EDT of ``alt`` w.r.t. ``ref`` via AcousticMetricsCallback.\\n    83\\t\\n    84\\t    ``P_ref`` is passed as the ground truth and ``P_alt`` as the prediction, so\\n    85\\t    the returned errors quantify how far the rotated run drifts from baseline.\\n    86\\t    FD / retrieval / env are disabled; no AGREE checkpoint is required.\\n    87\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    88\\t    callback = AcousticMetricsCallback(\\n    89\\t        dataset_name=\\\"AcousticRooms\\\",\\n    90\\t        sample_rate=sample_rate,\\n    91\\t        sample_size=sample_rate,\\n    92\\t        audio_channels=1,\\n    93\\t        eval_per_scene=False,\\n    94\\t        device=device,\\n    95\\t        eval_T60=True,\\n    96\\t        eval_C50=True,\\n    97\\t        eval_EDT=True,\\n    98\\t        AGREE_ckpt=None,\\n    99\\t    )\\n   100\\t    n = ref.shape[0]\\n   101\\t    for start in range(0, n, batch_size):\\n   102\\t        pred = alt[start:start + batch_size].float().to(device)\\n   103\\t        gt = ref[start:start + batch_size].float().to(device)\\n   104\\t        # scene=None follows the non-per-scene AR path; T60 then includes every sample.\\n   105\\t        callback.update_metrics(\\\"test\\\", pred, gt, scene=None)\\n   106\\t    metrics = callback.compute_metrics(\\\"test\\\")\\n   107\\t    return {key: _to_float(val) for key, val in metrics.items()}\\n   108\\t\\n   109\\t\\n   110\\tdef _load_raw(path: str):\\n   111\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"Load a prediction file, supporting both storage formats.\\n   112\\t\\n   113\\t    Returns ``(tensor, meta)`` where ``meta`` is ``None`` for the legacy bare\\n   114\\t    tensor file and the sidecar dict for the newer\\n   115\\t    ``{\\\"predictions\\\": tensor, \\\"meta\\\": {...}}`` format written by\\n   116\\t    ``eval_FLAC.py --store_predictions``. The tensor is returned un-normalised.\\n   117\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   118\\t    obj = torch.load(path, map_location=\\\"cpu\\\")\\n   119\\t    if isinstance(obj, dict) and \\\"predictions\\\" in obj:\\n   120\\t        return obj[\\\"predictions\\\"], obj.get(\\\"meta\\\")\\n   121\\t    return obj, None\\n   122\\t\\n   123\\t\\n   124\\tdef _normalise(tensor: Any, path: str) -> torch.Tensor:\\n   125\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"Validate a prediction tensor and normalise it to ``[N, 1, T]``.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   126\\t    if not isinstance(tensor, torch.Tensor):\\n   127\\t        raise TypeError(f\\\"{path} did not contain a tensor (got {type(tensor)})\\\")\\n   128\\t    if tensor.dim() == 2:  # [N, T] -> [N, 1, T]\\n   129\\t        tensor = tensor.unsqueeze(1)\\n   130\\t    if tensor.dim() != 3:\\n   131\\t        raise ValueError(f\\\"Expected a 2D/3D prediction tensor, got shape {tuple(tensor.shape)}\\\")\\n   132\\t    return tensor\\n   133\\t\\n   134\\t\\n   135\\tdef load_predictions(path: str) -> torch.Tensor:\\n   136\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"Load a stored prediction tensor and normalise it to ``[N, 1, T]``.\\n   137\\t\\n   138\\t    Accepts both the legacy bare-tensor file and the new sidecar dict\\n   139\\t    ``{\\\"predictions\\\": tensor, \\\"meta\\\": {...}}``; any meta is ignored here (use\\n   140\\t    :func:`load_prediction_meta` / :func:`guard_meta` for the consistency check).\\n   141\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   142\\t    tensor, _meta = _load_raw(path)\\n   143\\t    return _normalise(tensor, path)\\n   144\\t\\n   145\\t\\n   146\\tdef load_prediction_meta(path: str):\\n   147\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return the sidecar meta dict for a prediction file, or ``None`` (legacy).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   148\\t    _tensor, meta = _load_raw(path)\\n   149\\t    return meta\\n   150\\t\\n   151\\t\\n   152\\t# Keys that must match for the comparison to be meaningful: dataset_config /\\n   153\\t# seed / batch_size so sample i corresponds across the two files, plus\\n   154\\t# cond_method / frame_avg_angles / cond_autocast so both runs used the same\\n   155\\t# conditioning method and precision (a vanilla-vs-fa_invariant, different-angle\\n   156\\t# or fp16-vs-bf16 gap is a method/precision difference, not a Metric-1\\n   157\\t# invariance gap). ``rotate_deg`` is intentionally EXEMPT: comparing a rotated\\n   158\\t# run against an unrotated baseline is this tool's purpose.\\n   159\\t_GUARD_KEYS = (\\n   160\\t    \\\"dataset_config\\\", \\\"seed\\\", \\\"batch_size\\\", \\\"cond_method\\\", \\\"frame_avg_angles\\\",\\n   161\\t    \\\"cond_autocast\\\",\\n   162\\t    # exp_11: the orbit EXECUTION is part of the protocol. The batched path\\n   163\\t    # regroups the split's tail batch (and, in training, shares RoPE draws\\n   164\\t    # across a chunk), so a legacy-loop prediction set and a batched one are not\\n   165\\t    # interchangeable even at identical angles.\\n   166\\t    \\\"orbit_execution\\\", \\\"frame_avg_fwd_cap\\\", \\\"source_sha\\\",\\n   167\\t)\\n   168\\t\\n   169\\t\\n   170\\tdef _guard_value(meta: Dict[str, Any], key: str) -> Any:\\n   171\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"Guarded value with a legacy default: sidecars written before the\\n   172\\t    ``cond_autocast`` field existed factually ran the default autocast.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   173\\t    if key == \\\"cond_autocast\\\":\\n   174\\t        return meta.get(key, \\\"default\\\")\\n   175\\t    return meta.get(key)\\n   176\\t\\n   177\\t\\n   178\\tdef guard_meta(meta_ref, meta_alt, ref_path: str = \\\"ref\\\", alt_path: str = \\\"alt\\\") -> None:\\n   179\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"Hard-error when two runs' sidecar meta make their predictions incomparable.\\n   180\\t\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"label\":\"eval\",\"output\":\"24:``model.xRIR.apply_delay`` hard-codes ``.cuda()``, so the whole evaluation runs on GPU.\\n47:    per_sample_losses,\\n79:def forward_conditions(model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, k, aligned0):\\n91:        aligned0: the ``k = 0`` aligned reference audio, i.e.\\n102:        with fixed_alignment(model, aligned0):\\n114:            reference for ``consistency``.\\n118:        ``{\\\"loss\\\", \\\"stft\\\", \\\"decay\\\", \\\"log_mse\\\", \\\"consistency\\\"}``, each a detached CPU\\n121:        * ``loss``/``stft``/``decay`` -- ``tools.per_sample_metrics.per_sample_losses``,\\n123:        * ``log_mse`` -- the per-sample form of exp_01's\\n125:        * ``consistency`` -- ``mean |out_k - out_0|`` over the log-spectrogram, exactly\\n128:    loss, stft, decay = per_sample_losses(out_k, tgt_spec)\\n131:        log_mse = ((out_k[..., 0] - log_tgt) ** 2).flatten(1).mean(dim=1)\\n132:        consistency = (out_k - out_0).abs().flatten(1).mean(dim=1)\\n134:            \\\"log_mse\\\": log_mse.detach().cpu().float(),\\n135:            \\\"consistency\\\": consistency.detach().cpu().float()}\\n138:def acoustic_metrics_batch(out_k, tgt_wav, query_keys, evaluator, gl_seed):\\n209:def decomposition_at(model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, aligned0, k=32):\\n210:    \\\"\\\"\\\"Where a patch-aligned yaw enters an ``xRIR_Cyl`` forward pass (diagnostic).\\n212:    CylindricalViT's tokens are equivariant to patch-aligned azimuth rolls, but ``xRIR``\\n226:        k: the patch-aligned angle to decompose (the plan's diagnostic angle is 32).\\n263:        model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, 0, aligned0)[0]\\n265:        model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, k, aligned0)[0]\\n329:def pad_batch(batch, batch_size):\\n384:        batch_size: the canonical compute shape (see :func:`pad_batch`).  A short batch\\n394:    batch, n_real = pad_batch(batch, batch_size)\\n405:        aligned0 = model.shift_and_align(ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs)\\n415:            model, depth_coord, ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs, tgt_wav, k, aligned0)\\n422:                    acoustic_0 = acoustic_metrics_batch(pred, tgt_real, keys, evaluator,\\n426:                cell.update(acoustic_metrics_batch(pred, tgt_real, keys, evaluator, gl_seed))\\n470:        aligned0 = model.shift_and_align(ref_irs, src_loc, ref_locs)\\n472:                            aligned0, k=k)\\n535:    ``--batch-size`` rows (see :func:`pad_batch`), so a query's recorded values do not\\n551:        RuntimeError: if no GPU is visible (``model.xRIR.apply_delay`` hard-codes ``.cuda()``).\\n556:        raise RuntimeError(\\\"eval_yaw_rotation needs a GPU: xRIR.apply_delay is .cuda()-only\\\")\\n     1\\t\\\"\\\"\\\"Active yaw rotation of an xRIR scene about the receiver's vertical axis.\\n     2\\t\\n     3\\tExperiment exp_03 (``yaw_rotation_degradation``) asks whether jointly rotating the\\n     4\\treceiver-centred depth panorama and the source / reference-source coordinates in yaw\\n     5\\tdegrades RIR synthesis.  For an omnidirectional source and a monaural receiver this\\n     6\\trotation is a symmetry of the acoustics, so the ground-truth RIR is unchanged and any\\n     7\\tchange in the prediction is model sensitivity.\\n     8\\t\\n     9\\tConvention\\n    10\\t----------\\n    11\\tAn **active** yaw of the whole scene by ``+Delta`` about the receiver's ``z`` axis,\\n    12\\tright-handed (counter-clockwise seen from ``+z``: ``+x -> +y``), with\\n    13\\t\\n    14\\t    Delta = 2*pi*k / W\\n    15\\t\\n    16\\tfor an integer roll of ``k`` panorama columns (``W`` columns in total).  The panorama\\n    17\\tconvention is the dataset's ``convert_equirect_to_camera_coord``: column ``c`` looks\\n    18\\talong ``theta_c = (c + 0.5) * 2*pi/W - pi`` with ``x = d cos(phi) cos(theta)``,\\n    19\\t``y = d cos(phi) sin(theta)``, ``z = -d sin(phi)``.  Because ``theta_c - theta_{c-k} =\\n    20\\t2*pi*k/W = Delta``, rolling the panorama by ``+k`` columns and then rotating the xyz\\n    21\\tchannel vectors by ``Rz(Delta)`` is exactly the same scene as re-projecting the rolled\\n    22\\tdepth map, i.e. ``Rz(Delta) . roll(convert(D), k) == convert(roll(D, k))``.\\n    23\\t\\n    24\\tAll functions are pure (no in-place modification of their arguments) and work on CPU\\n    25\\tand CUDA tensors.\\n    26\\t\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    27\\tfrom __future__ import annotations\\n    28\\t\\n    29\\timport contextlib\\n    30\\timport math\\n    31\\timport numbers\\n    32\\timport types\\n    33\\tfrom typing import Tuple\\n    34\\t\\n    35\\timport torch\\n    36\\t\\n    37\\t\\n    38\\tdef _check_k_and_width(k, W) -> None:\\n    39\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"Validate a column roll ``k`` and a panorama width ``W``.\\n    40\\t\\n    41\\t    Raises:\\n    42\\t        TypeError: if ``k`` or ``W`` is not an integer (floats are rejected rather than\\n    43\\t            silently truncated, since a fractional roll has no meaning here).\\n    44\\t        ValueError: if ``W <= 0``.\\n    45\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    46\\t    if isinstance(k, bool) or not isinstance(k, numbers.Integral):\\n    47\\t        raise TypeError(\\\"k must be an integer number of panorama columns, got {!r}\\\".format(k))\\n    48\\t    if isinstance(W, bool) or not isinstance(W, numbers.Integral):\\n    49\\t        raise TypeError(\\\"W must be an integer panorama width, got {!r}\\\".format(W))\\n    50\\t    if int(W) <= 0:\\n    51\\t        raise ValueError(\\\"W must be positive, got {}\\\".format(W))\\n    52\\t\\n    53\\t\\n    54\\tdef _require_float(t: torch.Tensor, name: str) -> None:\\n    55\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"Raise ``TypeError`` unless ``t`` is a floating-point tensor.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    56\\t    if not torch.is_floating_point(t):\\n    57\\t        raise TypeError(\\\"{} must be a floating-point tensor, got dtype {}\\\".format(name, t.dtype))\\n    58\\t\\n    59\\t\\n    60\\tdef yaw_angle_rad(k: int, W: int = 512) -> float:\\n    61\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"Yaw angle in radians corresponding to a roll of ``k`` panorama columns.\\n    62\\t\\n    63\\t    Args:\\n    64\\t        k: integer number of panorama columns; may be negative or ``>= W`` (not reduced\\n    65\\t            here).  Non-integer values raise ``TypeError``.\\n    66\\t        W: integer panorama width in columns; must be positive.\\n    67\\t\\n    68\\t    Returns:\\n    69\\t        ``2 * pi * k / W`` as a Python float.\\n    70\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    71\\t    _check_k_and_width(k, W)\\n    72\\t    return 2.0 * math.pi * float(k) / float(W)\\n    73\\t\\n    74\\t\\n    75\\tdef rotate_vectors_z(v: torch.Tensor, angle: float) -> torch.Tensor:\\n    76\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"Actively rotate 3-D vectors about the ``z`` axis by ``angle`` radians.\\n    77\\t\\n    78\\t    ``x' = x cos(a) - y sin(a)``, ``y' = x sin(a) + y cos(a)``, ``z' = z``.\\n    79\\t\\n    80\\t    Args:\\n    81\\t        v: floating-point tensor of shape ``[..., 3]``; the last axis holds ``(x, y, z)``.\\n    82\\t            Integer tensors are rejected (the rotation is not closed over the integers).\\n    83\\t        angle: rotation angle in radians (right-handed about ``+z``).\\n    84\\t\\n    85\\t    Returns:\\n    86\\t        A new tensor of the same shape, dtype and device as ``v``.\\n    87\\t\\n    88\\t    Raises:\\n    89\\t        TypeError: if ``v`` is not floating point.\\n    90\\t        ValueError: if the last axis of ``v`` is not of size 3.\\n    91\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    92\\t    if v.shape[-1] != 3:\\n    93\\t        raise ValueError(\\\"rotate_vectors_z expects the last axis to be xyz (size 3), \\\"\\n    94\\t                         \\\"got shape {}\\\".format(tuple(v.shape)))\\n    95\\t    _require_float(v, \\\"v\\\")\\n    96\\t    cos_a = math.cos(angle)\\n    97\\t    sin_a = math.sin(angle)\\n    98\\t    x, y, z = v[..., 0], v[..., 1], v[..., 2]\\n    99\\t    return torch.stack([x * cos_a - y * sin_a, x * sin_a + y * cos_a, z], dim=-1)\\n   100\\t\\n   101\\t\\n   102\\tdef rotate_scene_yaw(\\n   103\\t    depth_coord: torch.Tensor,\\n   104\\t    src_loc: torch.Tensor,\\n   105\\t    ref_locs: torch.Tensor,\\n   106\\t    k: int,\\n   107\\t    W: int = 512,\\n   108\\t) -> Tuple[torch.Tensor, torch.Tensor, torch.Tensor]:\\n   109\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"Apply the active ``+2*pi*k/W`` yaw to a whole xRIR scene.\\n   110\\t\\n   111\\t    ``depth_coord' = Rz(Delta) . roll(depth_coord, k, dim=-1)``,\\n   112\\t    ``src_loc' = Rz(Delta) src_loc``, ``ref_locs' = Rz(Delta) ref_locs``.\\n   113\\t\\n   114\\t    Args:\\n   115\\t        depth_coord: receiver-frame panorama coordinates ``[B, 3, H, W]``, floating point.\\n   116\\t        src_loc: receiver-frame query-source position ``[B, 3]``, floating point.\\n   117\\t        ref_locs: receiver-frame reference-source positions ``[B, K, 3]``, floating point.\\n   118\\t        k: integer column roll; negative values and values ``>= W`` are reduced modulo\\n   119\\t            ``W``.  Non-integer values raise ``TypeError``.\\n   120\\t        W: integer panorama width in columns (must match ``depth_coord.shape[-1]``).\\n   121\\t\\n   122\\t    Returns:\\n   123\\t        ``(depth_coord', src_loc', ref_locs')`` -- new tensors; the inputs are untouched.\\n   124\\t\\n   125\\t    Raises:\\n   126\\t        TypeError: if ``k``/``W`` are not integers or any tensor is not floating point.\\n   127\\t        ValueError: if ``W <= 0`` or the shapes do not match the contract.\\n   128\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   129\\t    _check_k_and_width(k, W)\\n   130\\t    _require_float(depth_coord, \\\"depth_coord\\\")\\n   131\\t    _require_float(src_loc, \\\"src_loc\\\")\\n   132\\t    _require_float(ref_locs, \\\"ref_locs\\\")\\n   133\\t    if depth_coord.dim() != 4 or depth_coord.shape[1] != 3:\\n   134\\t        raise ValueError(\\\"depth_coord must have shape [B, 3, H, W], got {}\\\".format(\\n   135\\t            tuple(depth_coord.shape)))\\n   136\\t    if depth_coord.shape[-1] != W:\\n   137\\t        raise ValueError(\\\"depth_coord has {} columns but W={}\\\".format(\\n   138\\t            depth_coord.shape[-1], W))\\n   139\\t\\n   140\\t    k = int(k) % int(W)\\n   141\\t    angle = yaw_angle_rad(k, W)\\n   142\\t\\n   143\\t    rolled = torch.roll(depth_coord, shifts=k, dims=-1)          # [B, 3, H, W]\\n   144\\t    depth_rot = rotate_vectors_z(rolled.permute(0, 2, 3, 1), angle).permute(0, 3, 1, 2)\\n   145\\t    return depth_rot.contiguous(), rotate_vectors_z(src_loc, angle), rotate_vectors_z(ref_locs, angle)\\n   146\\t\\n   147\\t\\n   148\\tdef integer_delays(\\n   149\\t    src_loc: torch.Tensor,\\n   150\\t    ref_locs: torch.Tensor,\\n   151\\t    sr: int = 22050,\\n   152\\t    c: float = 343.0,\\n   153\\t) -> torch.Tensor:\\n   154\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"Integer direct-path delays exactly as ``model.xRIR.xRIR.shift_and_align`` computes them.\\n   155\\t\\n   156\\t    The op sequence and dtypes mirror the model line for line\\n   157\\t    (``torch.linalg.norm`` -> ``torch.round(...).int()``), so both the values and the\\n   158\\t    dtype agree bit for bit with the model's ``delay_unit`` on the same inputs.\\n   159\\t\\n   160\\t    Args:\\n   161\\t        src_loc: receiver-frame query-source position ``[B, 3]``.\\n   162\\t        ref_locs: receiver-frame reference-source positions ``[B, K, 3]``.\\n   163\\t        sr: sample rate in Hz (the model hard-codes 22050).\\n   164\\t        c: speed of sound in m/s (the model hard-codes 343).\\n   165\\t\\n   166\\t    Returns:\\n   167\\t        ``[B, K]`` **int32** tensor of sample delays -- the dtype the model itself produces\\n   168\\t        (positive = reference arrives earlier than the query source and is therefore\\n   169\\t        shifted right).\\n   170\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   171\\t    dist_src = torch.linalg.norm(src_loc, dim=1).unsqueeze(1)          # [B, 1]\\n   172\\t    dist_ref = torch.linalg.norm(ref_locs, dim=-1)                     # [B, K]\\n   173\\t    return torch.round((dist_src - dist_ref) / c * sr).int()           # as in shift_and_align\\n   174\\t\\n   175\\t\\n   176\\t@contextlib.contextmanager\\n   177\\tdef fixed_alignment(model, aligned_refs: torch.Tensor):\\n   178\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"Temporarily pin ``model.shift_and_align`` to a pre-computed alignment.\\n   179\\t\\n   180\\t    ``xRIR.shift_and_align`` is algebraically yaw-invariant but not numerically: float32\\n   181\\t    norms plus ``round()`` can flip a delay by one sample under rotation.  The primary\\n   182\\t    experimental condition therefore holds the direct-path alignment at the ``k = 0``\\n   183\\t    coordinates so that only the geometry branches see the rotation::\\n   184\\t\\n   185\\t        aligned = model.shift_and_align(refs, src0, ref_locs0)\\n   186\\t        with fixed_alignment(model, aligned):\\n   187\\t            out, tgt = model(depth_k, refs, src_k, ref_locs_k, tgt)\\n   188\\t\\n   189\\t    The replacement is installed as a *bound method* of ``model`` (so ``model``'s own\\n   190\\t    ``forward`` sees the usual ``self.shift_and_align(x, src, ref)`` call signature), and\\n   191\\t    the original bound method is restored on exit, including when the block raises.\\n   192\\t\\n   193\\t    This mutates ``model`` for the duration of the block: it is for **serial, eager**\\n   194\\t    evaluation only and is **not thread-safe** -- do not share ``model`` across threads,\\n   195\\t    and do not let a lazily evaluated forward escape the block.\\n   196\\t\\n   197\\t    Args:\\n   198\\t        model: an ``xRIR`` (or subclass) instance.\\n   199\\t        aligned_refs: the cached ``[B, K, T]`` aligned reference audio to return instead.\\n   200\\t\\n   201\\t    Yields:\\n   202\\t        ``model``, with its ``shift_and_align`` replaced.\\n   203\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   204\\t    had_own = \\\"shift_and_align\\\" in vars(model)\\n   205\\t    original = vars(model).get(\\\"shift_and_align\\\", None)\\n   206\\t\\n   207\\t    model.shift_and_align = types.MethodType(\\n   208\\t        lambda self, x, src_loc, ref_ir_locs: aligned_refs, model)\\n   209\\t    try:\\n   210\\t        yield model\\n   211\\t    finally:\\n   212\\t        if had_own:\\n   213\\t            model.shift_and_align = original\\n   214\\t        elif \\\"shift_and_align\\\" in vars(model):\\n   215\\t            # Guarded so a raise inside the block is never masked by an AttributeError here.\\n   216\\t            del model.shift_and_align\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"label\":\"announce\",\"output\":\"\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0dfa7-77ce-7782-ac15-668bcbd12958","create_time":1790458714.1521616}}}
